806 0 obj
<<
/Title (["That", "is", "a", "cute", "dog"]. Subword tokenization allows models to handle unknown or complex words by breaking them into smaller meaningful fragments.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 800 0 R
/Next 811 0 R
>>
endobj
807 0 obj
<<
/Title (Using these neural networks, word2vec generates word embeddings by looking at which other words they tend to appear next to in a given sentence. We start by assigning every word in our vocabulary with a vector embedding, say of 50 values for each word initialized with random values. Then in every training step, as illustrated in Figure 1-7, we take pairs of words from the training data and a model attempts to predict whether or not they are likely to be neighbors in a sentence)
/Dest [24 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 812 0 R
/Next 801 0 R
>>
endobj
808 0 obj
<<
/G3 347 0 R
>>
endobj
809 0 obj
<<
/F9 359 0 R
>>
endobj
810 0 obj
<<
/Type /Group
/S /Transparency
/I true
>>
endobj
811 0 obj
<<
/Title (2. Embeddings \(Converting Words into Numbers\) Once text is tokenized, each token is mapped to a dense numerical vector called an embedding. Embeddings place words into a high-dimensional mathematical space where words with similar meanings are located closer together.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 806 0 R
/Next 813 0 R
>>
endobj
812 0 obj
<<
/Title (Better Representations with Dense Vector Embeddings)
/Dest [23 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 814 0 R
/First 815 0 R
/Last 816 0 R
/Count -3
/Next 807 0 R
>>
endobj
813 0 obj
<<
/Title (Static vs. Contextual Embeddings: Early embedding algorithms like word2vec assigned a single static vector to each word. Modern Language AI uses contextualized embeddings, where the vector representation of a word dynamically changes depending on the surrounding sentence. For instance, the word "bank" receives a different numerical vector in "river bank" versus "investment bank")
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 811 0 R
/Next 817 0 R
>>
endobj
814 0 obj
<<
/Title <>
/Dest [22 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 818 0 R
/Next 812 0 R
>>
endobj
815 0 obj
<<
/Title (Bag-of-words, although an elegant approach, has a flaw. It considers language to be nothing more than an almost literal bag of words and ignores the semantic nature, or meaning, of text.)
/Dest [23 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 812 0 R
/Next 819 0 R
>>
endobj
816 0 obj
<<
/Title (To generate these semantic representations, word2vec leverages neural networks. These networks consist of interconnected layers of nodes that process information. As illustrated in Figure 1-6, neural networks can have many layers where each connection has a certain weight depending on the input. These weights are often referred to as the parameters of the model.)
/Dest [23 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 812 0 R
/Prev 819 0 R
>>
endobj
817 0 obj
<<
/Title (3. The Transformer Architecture & Self-Attention Most modern Language AI systems rely on the Transformer architecture. The core innovation of the Transformer is the self-attention mechanism, which allows the model to evaluate how every word in a sequence relates to every other word.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 813 0 R
/Next 820 0 R
>>
endobj
818 0 obj
<<
/Title (Using our vocabulary, we simply count how often a word in each sentence appears, quite literally creating a bag of words. As a result, a bag-of-words model aims to create representations of text in the form of numbers, also called vectors or vector representations, observed in Figure 1-5. Throughout the course, we refer to these kinds of models as representation models)
/Dest [21 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 821 0 R
/Next 814 0 R
>>
endobj
819 0 obj
<<
/Title (Released in 2013, word2vec was one of the first successful attempts at capturing the meaning of text in embeddings. Embeddings are vector representations of data that attempt to capture its meaning. To do so, word2vec learns semantic representations of words by training on vast amounts of textual data, like the entirety of Wikipedia.)
/Dest [23 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 812 0 R
/Prev 815 0 R
/Next 816 0 R
>>
endobj
820 0 obj
<<
/Title (Example: Consider the prompt: "The dog chased the squirrel because it...". To predict what word comes next, the model must determine what "it" refers to. The self-attention mechanism calculates relevance scores across the tokens and heavily connects "it" to "squirrel" \(or "dog"\), incorporating that context into its final decision.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 817 0 R
/Next 822 0 R
>>
endobj
821 0 obj
<<
/Title (The most common method for tokenization is by splitting on a whitespace to create individual words. However, this has its disadvantages as some languages, like Man-darin, do not have whitespaces around individual words. In the next chapter, we will go in depth about tokenization and how that technique influences language models. As illustrated in Figure 1-4, after tokenization, we combine all unique words from each sentence to create a vocabulary that we can use to represent the sentences.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 823 0 R
/Next 818 0 R
>>
endobj
822 0 obj
<<
/Title (4. Autoregressive Prediction & Generation Generative Language AI models are completion machines. They operate autoregressively, meaning they generate output text one token at a time. At each step, the model computes a probability distribution over its entire vocabulary to pick the most likely next token, appends that token to its context, and repeats the loop.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 820 0 R
/Next 824 0 R
>>
endobj
823 0 obj
<<
/Title (Representing Language as a Bag-of-Words)
/Dest [19 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 825 0 R
/First 826 0 R
/Last 826 0 R
/Count -1
/Next 821 0 R
>>
endobj
824 0 obj
<<
/Title (Example: If given the input prompt "So long and thanks for", the model calculates probabilities for possible next words \(e.g., 44% probability for "all", 33% for "the"\), selects "all", adds it to the prompt, and then predicts the following token \("the"\).)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 822 0 R
/Next 825 0 R
>>
endobj
825 0 obj
<<
/Title (A Recent History of Language AI)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 824 0 R
/First 827 0 R
/Last 827 0 R
/Count -1
/Next 823 0 R
>>
endobj
826 0 obj
<<
/Title <>
/Dest [19 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 823 0 R
>>
endobj
827 0 obj
<<
/Title (Language, however, is a tricky concept for computers. Text is unstructured in nature and loses its meaning when represented by zeros and ones \(individual characters\). As a result, throughout the history of Language AI, there has been a large focus on representing language in a structured manner so that it can more easily be used by computers. Examples of these Language AI tasks are provided in Figure 1-2.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 825 0 R
>>
endobj
